rs  down the road uh in in a couple of  decades we have to focus all our  energies and resources on a particular  region of the world  uh that that's  you know the nature of our mission but  you do understand there are a lot of  voices from uh countries where they have  developing economies who say that's just  unfair we're not being treated the same  way as europe do you have do you  understand that position  they don't need the support at the  moment but what the entire world is  is stability  if you have a big chunk of the global  economy that is in this array and that  is not being supported and that where  stability is not restored it's hardship  for everybody well egypt for example  would be an example uh  in spite of the political difficulties  obviously that's a country that would be  in dire need of help and and the imf  so far  has not given any loans where were we  there  you know what is  absolutely needed uh for a partnership  with the imf to work is  large political support  and  very strong dedication of the team to  actually enforce the discipline  and make the reforms that are needed to  restore the situation to restore  stability and to create jobs for the  people  as soon as that is there  we are ready to jump we've worked a lot  with the egyptian people over the last  few months and we will continue to do so  believe me  talking about jumping let's go to this  side of the atlantic we as we record  this interview we do have a government  shutdown here in the united states would  you say that's more on the side of  necessary  or more on the side of foolish  i think it's it's bad news  and if it wasn't bad enough the  discussions  currently about the debt ceiling are are  even worse and could have very very  damaging effect uh not only on the u.s  economy but on the global economy  so we are very concerned about about the  uh that potential risk i have  huge trust that this will not happen why  do you have that trust  with the political consolation 